Task 1:
///Lab: Username enumeration via different responses///

Open the site in burp and try to log in with anything...like with the username: xyz and password : zyx.
In http history you will see a post request:
POST /login HTTP/2
Host: 0a74002304f50ab3810ddfd8000e000d.web-security-academy.net
Cookie: session=OgaVZFPKecxSabev6pOqkCOe3rVI9Zqc
...

username=asd&password=asd

Send this request to intruder and also to repeater.
On the intruder panel add a position to the username. In the labs desription there are a little database about usernames and passwords:
"Candidate usernames
Candidate passwords"

Open the usernames, copy them out and paste them into the payload configuration, then go to settings/Grep - Match and add this: Incorrect password. When we tried to log in we got an error:"invalid username".
So if the username is right, but the password is not correct we will get another error message back, which will be probably incorrect password...and start the attack.
To me the username 'an' was a correct username, but the password was incorrect (i checked in repeater)...now change our payload...delete the username position and copy what username your burp found, add a position to the password value
copy out all the passwords from the database, delete the grep - match and run the attack. If you check the 'Length' column you will see that most of the passwords are more than 6000, there will be only one
which is only like 180. That is our password. It was 'michael' to me. Log in with the username and password (an/michael to me), and the lab is solved.

